      "math_academy_id": "q-320743",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Write $\\frac{8{x}^{5}-9}{8{x}^{5}+4}$ in the form $a+\\frac{b}{8{x}^{5}+4},$ where $a\\ne 0.$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td width=\"25\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">$8{x}^{5}-9$</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" width=\"25\" align=\"center\">$=$</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" width=\"25\" align=\"center\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" width=\"25\" align=\"center\">$+$</td>\n<td width=\"50\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"25\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">$8{x}^{5}+4$</td>\n<td width=\"25\" height=\"25\" align=\"center\">$8{x}^{5}+4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "First, we add and subtract $4$ in the numerator. This reveals a copy of the denominator without changing the value of the expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8{x}^{5}-9}{8{x}^{5}+4}\\,=\\,\\frac{8{x}^{5}+4-4-9}{8{x}^{5}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we split the fraction into two, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8{x}^{5}+4-4-9}{8{x}^{5}+4} & =\\frac{8{x}^{5}+4}{8{x}^{5}+4}+\\frac{-4-9}{8{x}^{5}+4} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{-13}{8{x}^{5}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
